nk we're  you know is the is the government being  forced to be more respectful of people's  rights or you know is that something  that's always kind of a no-man's land  battle that's just yeah going back it  really it really is the pendulum swings  back and forth and I you know like that  the GPS case that I mentioned you know  all so many of those cases are four to  three and again even at the grassroots  level you have people totally willing to  violate other people's civil liberties  and insisting that their own be be  recognized this is where I think that  libertarians can play a very very  significant role and that is to show  that if your free speech rights are  violated my free speech rights are  violated if your due process rights are  violated so our mind that we have a  universality when it comes to the  protection of Rights and frankly there's  no one else in the society right now  that I think has neutral principles that  they will apply in all all instances do  you enjoy being a Supreme Court justice  I absolutely love it you know I loved  being a litigator but at this stage of  my career I have lots of people I've  trained and mentored over the years who  are doing the litigation stuff and I  love bringing my experience to bear and  working with six other people who are  extraordinarily bright extraordinarily  well meaning to reach in many instances  a collegial decision  you

